We know that $\sin(x+y) = \sin(x)\cos(y) + \cos(x)\sin(y)$. So we have \begin{align\*}\sin(x + y) &\leq \sin(x) + \sin(y) \\ \sin(x)\cos(y) + \cos(x)\sin(y) &\leq \sin(x) + \sin(y)\end{align\*}Because this holds for every $x$ between $0$ and $\pi$, inclusive, by letting $x = 0$ we have \begin{align \*}\sin(y) &\leq \sin(y)  \end{align\*}So for this inequality to be true, we require \begin{align\*}\sin(y) &\leq 0\end{align\*}Because $y \in [0,\pi]$, we have $y \in [0, \pi] \cap [0,\pi] = [0,\pi]$ is where $\sin(y) \leq 0$, which means that all $y \in [0,\pi]$ for which this inequality holds true satisfy this condition and the $\sin(x + y) \leq \sin(x) + \sin(y)$ is a given condition so the solution is any $y \in [0,\pi]$. We conclude that the answer is $\boxed{[0,\pi]}$.

